<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<phpunit xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
         xsi:noNamespaceSchemaLocation="https://schema.phpunit.de/10.5/phpunit.xsd"
         bootstrap="bootstrap.php"
         cacheDirectory=".phpunit.cache"
         beStrictAboutOutputDuringTests="true"
         failOnRisky="true"
         failOnWarning="true"
         failOnSkipped="true">
    <testsuites>
        <testsuite name="unit">
            <directory>tests/Unit</directory>
        </testsuite>
        <testsuite name="integration">
            <directory>tests/Integration</directory>
        </testsuite>
    </testsuites>
    <!--
        Three groups are excluded from both suites by default: their tests depend on an external
        tool or on a privilege that is not present on every machine. Such a dependency is
        expressed as a group excluded by configuration rather than as a conditional
        markTestSkipped(): under failOnSkipped (below) a skip is indistinguishable from a green
        run that verified nothing.

        mariadb-client — the tests start a container with real MariaDB clients
        (docker run mariadb:10.11); docker is not available everywhere. It is run by requesting
        the group explicitly: vendor/bin/phpunit with the testsuite (integration) and group
        (mariadb-client) options.

        mysql-client-tools (SnapshotFormatTest) and mysql-user-table (FixtureDatabaseUserTest) —
        these need real mysql/mysqldump clients in PATH and, respectively, the SELECT privilege on
        mysql.user. Unlike mariadb-client, CI provides both through the job's regular steps rather
        than through separate preparation for these groups: the clients are installed by the step
        "the mysql/mysqldump clients must be present on the runner"
        (.github/workflows/tests.yml), and the account is root. That is why the integration and
        integration-min-modx jobs run composer test:integration:with-clients rather than
        composer test:integration — the same suite, but with the exclude group option set to
        mariadb-client instead of the exclusion list from this file (docker is still not up in CI,
        so mariadb-client is the one that stays outside).

        The exception is the integration-mariadb-client job: it deliberately leaves the runner the
        clients ONLY under the names mariadb/mariadb-dump, while the mysql-client-tools group
        requires the pair mysqldump/mysql (the names MysqlDumper calls by default). That job
        therefore runs the plain composer test:integration — with the exclusion list FROM THIS
        file. The same set cannot be expressed portably on the command line: on PHPUnit 10.5.36 a
        repeated exclude group option keeps only the last group, and on 12.5.33 a comma-separated
        list is not parsed (measured, see ExcludeGroupOptionTest).

        The fragile part of this arrangement: exclude group REPLACES the XML exclusion list
        wholesale instead of adding to it. So if one MORE group is added to <groups><exclude> in
        the future (say, for another tool missing from CI), test:integration:with-clients will
        silently start including it too — both places will have to be updated together, not just
        this file.

        Options are written here without dashes throughout: XML forbids a double dash inside a
        comment.
    -->
    <groups>
        <exclude>
            <group>mariadb-client</group>
            <group>mysql-client-tools</group>
            <group>mysql-user-table</group>
        </exclude>
    </groups>
    <source>
        <include>
            <directory>src</directory>
        </include>
    </source>
</phpunit>
